Subject: Report whether an enum is scoped

`enum class E { foo };` and `enum E { foo };` produce the same generated
Rust - a type and a constant per enumerator - so nothing in the output
says which of the two C++ declared. The difference is not cosmetic: an
unscoped enumerator is a member of the scope enclosing the enum, and
hides anything of that name inherited into it, while a scoped one is
reachable only as `E::foo` and hides nothing. A generator emitting C++ of
its own has to know which, and has no way to find out: libclang answers
with `clang_EnumDecl_isScoped`, which `clang.rs` does not wrap.

Wrap it, carry the answer on `Enum` beside the representation and the
variants, and report it through a callback for the enums codegen writes
out. Off by default like every other `denote_*`, and the generated
bindings are unchanged.

